WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.502
A running total is another common window-function pattern.

00:00:03.502 --> 00:00:11.672
Instead of collapsing sales by date, we can keep each date or row in sequence while showing the

00:00:11.672 --> 00:00:11.997
cumulative revenue up to that point.

00:00:11.997 --> 00:00:17.107
The ORDER BY inside the window defines the progression of the calculation.

00:00:17.107 --> 00:00:22.712
Run the example and watch the running total increase as the dates move forward.

00:00:22.712 --> 00:00:30.637
This pattern appears in financial balances, cumulative enrollment, inventory movement, project progress,

00:00:30.637 --> 00:00:33.492
and many other time-ordered analyses.

00:00:33.492 --> 00:00:38.267
When you build a running total, make sure the ordering is deterministic.

00:00:38.267 --> 00:00:44.652
If multiple rows can share the same date, think about whether you need an additional ordering column to

00:00:44.652 --> 00:00:51.072
define a stable sequence. Window functions calculate across related rows without collapsing them.

00:00:51.072 --> 00:00:57.167
That lets you keep row-level detail while adding rank, running totals, or group averages.

00:00:57.167 --> 00:01:04.247
Always define the partition and ordering carefully because those clauses describe which rows belong together

00:01:04.247 --> 00:01:05.602
and in what sequence.
